We still have to see if $W\subset\widetilde W$. For the opposite inclusion we had to make restrictions \inserted{(in terms of $W$)} on the $I$'s (condition 2), now to get $\widetilde W$ large enough, i.e. $TC$ large enough, we must make assumptions which will ensure that this bunch of $I$'s is large enough --- a natural condition would be that it contains a separating interval. We know already there \deleted{are} \inserted{are} ``eligible'' \uline{separating} intervals, namely $W$-aspheric over $e$ and separating --- such as $L$.
\margin{but this cannot possibly be enough, as it would (taking just $L$ by itself) \deleted{\unsure{it}} give just one possibility \deleted{\unsure{of}} of $F$, hence one \unsure{fixed} particular $W$...}

Let $f\in W$, consider factorization
\[
X\overset{\deleted{\unsure{TC_0}}\inserted{i}}{\longrightarrow}X'\overset{p}{\unsure{-|\longrightarrow}}Y\qquad i\in TC,\ p\in F\quad(\text{or }i\in C,\ p\in TF)
\]
we know already that $i\in W$ (resp. $p\in W$) \deleted{Therefore} $p\in W$ (resp. $i\in W$) --- hence we are reduced to \deleted{the case of $i$} proving either one of the two relations
\[
\begin{aligned}
F\cap W&\subset TF&&\text{(necessary for }W\subset\widetilde W,\text{ as }TF=F\cap\widetilde W)\\
C\cap W&\subset TC&&\text{(id)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which are \deleted{\unsure{only}} equivalent \deleted{\unsure{to the}} clearly to the condition $W\subset\widetilde W$ (or equivalently \deleted{\unsure{W}} $\widetilde W=W$). The second \inserted{[first!]} means that if $f:X-Y$ \inserted{[is in $W$ and]} has the \deleted{\unsure{right}} RLP with respect to $(TC)_0$ i.e. all \inserted{to all} $\unsure{h|\alpha}(\deleted{\unsure{f}}i_0,I,e)$, then it has the RLP even with respect to \uline{all} monomorphisms which are in $W$.
\margin{[????]}

We would like this to hold at least in the following two cases:

\margin{In both cases $\mathcal A=\widehat A$}

a) $W=\underline W_A$ ($\underline W$ a basic localizer), $A$ a (local!) $\underline W$-test category

b) $A=A_0\times T$, \inserted{$\widehat A\simeq\underline{\operatorname{Hom}}(A_0^{\mathrm{opp}},\widehat T)\simeq(A_0\deleted{\unsure{\times}}T)^\wedge$} with $T$ a test category, $W=\underline W_{A/A_0}$ ($\underline W$ a basic localizer), $\underline W_{A/A_0}$ is defined componentwise via $\underline W_T$.
